    const val LINKING = """
however|to show contrast with the sentence before|Usually starts a new sentence, followed by a comma. More formal than "but".|The hotel was expensive. However, the service was excellent.|I'd love to come. However, I have to work.|We tried hard. The result, however, was disappointing.
on the other hand|to give the other side of an argument|Often after "on the one hand". Starts a sentence or follows a semicolon.|The job pays well. On the other hand, the hours are long.|On the one hand, cities are exciting; on the other hand, they're noisy.|Renting is flexible. On the other hand, you never own anything.
although|to show contrast inside one sentence|Followed by subject + verb. It can start the sentence.|Although it was raining, we went for a walk.|I enjoyed the film, although it was a bit long.|Although she's only 20, she runs her own business.
even though|a stronger "although": the fact is surprising|Followed by subject + verb. Never "even although".|Even though he was tired, he kept working.|She went to work even though she was ill.|Even though I've lived here for years, I still get lost.
despite|to show contrast (a preposition)|Followed by a noun or -ing, not subject + verb. Never "despite of".|Despite the rain, the match went ahead.|Despite being tired, she finished the race.|He got the job despite his lack of experience.
in spite of|the same as "despite"|Followed by a noun or -ing. Don't forget "of".|In spite of the traffic, we arrived on time.|She smiled in spite of feeling nervous.|In spite of everything, we're still friends.
while|to contrast two facts; or "during the time that"|For contrast it often starts the sentence: "While I agree, …".|While I understand your point, I disagree.|My sister loves sport, while I prefer reading.|He called while I was having a shower.
as a result|to show what happened because of something|Usually starts a new sentence, followed by a comma. "as a result of" + noun.|He didn't study. As a result, he failed.|The roads were icy. As a result, there were many accidents.|Flights were cancelled as a result of the storm.
therefore|to show a logical result (formal)|After a full stop or semicolon, or before the main verb: "We therefore decided …".|The shop was closed; therefore, we went home.|She's the most experienced, and therefore the best person for the job.|Prices rose. We therefore decided to wait.
consequently|the same as "as a result" (formal)|Starts a new sentence, followed by a comma.|He missed the train. Consequently, he was late for the interview.|The company lost money and, consequently, closed two shops.|Sales fell. Consequently, staff were laid off.
so that|to give a purpose|Followed by subject + can / could / would.|I left early so that I could catch the bus.|Speak clearly so that everyone can hear you.|She saved money so that she could travel.
in order to|to give a purpose (more formal than "to")|Followed by the infinitive. Negative: "in order not to".|She moved to London in order to find work.|In order to apply, you need a passport.|He spoke quietly in order not to wake the baby.
due to|to give a cause (formal)|Followed by a noun: "due to the weather".|The flight was delayed due to fog.|Due to illness, the concert has been cancelled.|His success is due to hard work.
because of|to give a cause|Followed by a noun or pronoun, not subject + verb.|We stayed in because of the rain.|Because of you, I missed the bus!|The road is closed because of an accident.
since|because (when the reason is already clear)|At the start of a sentence it usually means "because". It is also used for time.|Since you're here, can you help me?|Since it's late, let's take a taxi.|Since nobody objected, we went ahead.
as long as|on condition that|Followed by the present tense when it means the future.|You can borrow my car as long as you're careful.|I'll come as long as I'm back by ten.|As long as we work together, we'll finish on time.
unless|if not|Followed by a positive verb: "unless you hurry" = "if you don't hurry".|We'll miss the train unless we hurry.|Don't call me unless it's urgent.|Unless it rains, we'll have a picnic.
provided that|only if (formal)|Like "as long as": present tense for the future.|You can go out provided that you finish your homework.|Provided that the weather is good, the race will start at 10.|We'll sign the contract provided that the price is right.
in case|because something might happen|Not the same as "if": you do it now, before anything happens.|Take an umbrella in case it rains.|I'll write down my number in case you need it.|Save your work often in case the computer crashes.
otherwise|if not; or else|After a full stop, a semicolon or "or".|Hurry up, otherwise we'll be late.|Write it down; otherwise you'll forget.|It's lucky you called — otherwise I'd still be waiting.
moreover|to add a stronger point (formal)|Starts a sentence, followed by a comma. Mostly in writing.|The plan is too expensive. Moreover, it won't work.|The flat is cheap. Moreover, it's in a great area.|The report was late; moreover, it was full of mistakes.
in addition|to add information|"In addition, …" or "in addition to" + noun / -ing.|In addition, the hotel offers free parking.|In addition to English, she speaks French and German.|In addition to working full-time, he studies at night.
as well as|to add (and also)|Followed by a noun or -ing: "as well as studying".|She plays the guitar as well as the piano.|As well as being clever, he's very kind.|We visited Rome as well as Florence.
not only … but also|to stress two points|At the start of a sentence the verb comes first: "Not only is it cheap, but …".|She's not only clever but also very funny.|Not only did he forget my birthday, but he also forgot our anniversary.|The course is not only useful but also enjoyable.
for instance|to give an example|The same as "for example". Put commas around it.|Some animals, for instance bats, sleep during the day.|There are many ways to save money. For instance, you could cook at home.|Many English words come from French, for instance "restaurant".
such as|to give examples|Followed by a noun or a list. No comma after "such as".|I love fruit such as mangoes and kiwis.|Cities such as Rome and Paris are very popular.|Avoid foods such as sweets and crisps.
in other words|to say the same thing more simply|Followed by a comma.|He's "between jobs" — in other words, he's unemployed.|The price is negotiable. In other words, make me an offer.|The project is behind schedule; in other words, it's late.
in fact|to add a surprising or more exact fact|It often corrects or strengthens what came before.|I don't mind the rain. In fact, I quite like it.|He says he's busy, but in fact he's watching TV.|It's not expensive — in fact, it's free.
actually|to correct someone or add a surprising fact|It does NOT mean "now" (that's "currently").|Actually, I've changed my mind.|"You're Spanish?" "Actually, I'm Italian."|I actually enjoyed the exam.
first of all|to introduce the first point|Followed by a comma. Go on with "secondly", "finally".|First of all, I'd like to thank you all for coming.|First of all, let's look at the costs.|First of all, check that the machine is off.
finally|to introduce the last point|Followed by a comma. "At last" is different: after a long wait.|Finally, I'd like to talk about the future.|Finally, add the salt and serve.|Finally, remember to save your work.
in conclusion|to introduce the final summary (formal)|Mostly in essays and talks; followed by a comma.|In conclusion, social media has both benefits and risks.|In conclusion, I believe the law should change.|In conclusion, thank you all for listening.
as far as I'm concerned|to give your personal opinion|At the start or end of a sentence, with a comma.|As far as I'm concerned, the meeting was a waste of time.|You can do what you like, as far as I'm concerned.|As far as I'm concerned, family comes first.
on the whole|generally; considering everything|Usually at the start, followed by a comma.|On the whole, I enjoyed the course.|The weather was good on the whole.|On the whole, people here are very friendly.
apart from|except for; or in addition to|Followed by a noun or -ing.|Apart from the weather, the holiday was perfect.|Apart from English, she speaks Arabic.|Nobody knew, apart from my mother.
as soon as|immediately after|Followed by the present tense for the future, not "will".|Call me as soon as you arrive.|As soon as I saw her, I knew something was wrong.|I'll pay you as soon as I get paid.
by the time|not later than the moment when|Often with the past perfect or future perfect.|By the time we arrived, the film had started.|By the time you read this, I'll have left.|By the time he was 30, he had three children.
meanwhile|at the same time; in the time between|At the start of a sentence, followed by a comma.|I'll cook. Meanwhile, you can set the table.|She went to university. Meanwhile, her brother started working.|The bus is late. Meanwhile, it's starting to rain.
whether|to talk about two possibilities|"whether or not", "whether … or …". After a preposition use "whether", not "if".|I don't know whether he's coming.|Whether you like it or not, we're leaving.|It depends on whether we have enough money.
"""

